# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — Handoff Contracts

A handoff is the artifact that crosses a group boundary. Naming it removes the most common failure in
a multi-agent org: the receiving role re-derives what the sending role already knew, and the two
answers differ.

Each contract below is **what is passed**, not a description of the work. If the artifact is missing a
field, the receiver rejects the handoff rather than guessing.

---

## Studio → Design System · *gap request*
Raised when a screen needs something the DS does not have.

```yaml
gap:      component | token | variant | mode
need:     what the screen requires, in DS vocabulary
evidence: the screen + the state that needs it
blocking: which story is stalled
proposal: a suggested shape — a suggestion, not a decision
```
**Never** a code- or Figma-side local invention. A local fix is a second source of truth, and it will
diverge silently.

## Design System → Engineering · *published version*
```yaml
version:     the published library version
changes:     added / changed / deprecated
migration:   what consumers must do, per breaking change
tokens:      new or renamed, with old → new
verified_by: G1 parity result
```

## Studio → App Builder · *validated screen spec*
```yaml
screen:              name + the flow it belongs to
acceptance_criteria: observable, per state
states:              default · loading · empty · error · success
offline:             the offline path
copy:                string keys — approved, G4 signed
components:          which DS components compose it
motion:              per interaction — trigger · what moves · duration+easing TOKEN ·
                     interrupt behavior · reduced-motion alternative
verified_by:         G0
```
The prototype is the spec. `developer` ports **native concerns, not pixels** — re-drawing a validated
layout re-opens decisions that were already settled with evidence.

## App Builder → App Publisher · *release candidate*
```yaml
commit:          the exact SHA
changes:         the change list, per platform
device_checks:   what was verified on real hardware, and on what
regression:      baseline diff result
known_issues:    what is shipping unfixed, and why
verified_by:     G2 + G3
```

## App Publisher → Marketing · *shipped build*
```yaml
version:   the shipped version + track
live_at:   when it reached users
changes:   user-observable changes only — internal work is not a marketing input
assets:    store screenshot frames available
```

## Marketing → App Publisher · *listing update*
```yaml
fields:      title · subtitle · keywords · description
copy:        the text, per locale — G4 signed
assets:      screenshots, preview video
char_counts: counted BEFORE the call, per field limit
```
`marketing-lead` authors, `store-manager` publishes, and **confirms by re-reading the store** rather
than trusting the write's response.

## Operations → Leadership · *cycle read*
```yaml
crash_free:  rate, from the live console
reviews:     triaged themes + volume
funnel:      activation / retention movement
costs:       spend against budget
asks:        what needs a decision
verified_by: G7
```

---

## The rule that makes handoffs cheap

> The sending role states what it **verified**, not what it **believes**. The receiving role trusts
> verified claims and re-checks nothing else.

Without this, every boundary duplicates the work on both sides — and when the two results disagree,
nobody knows which to believe.
